  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\n  We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by developing a multi-resolution optimization strategy combined with a physics-inspired annealing process. This approach aims to coordinate local and global searching for an optimal binary solution within the given constraints. Unlike the previous programming attempt, we focus explicitly on the binary nature of the optimal solution and use a controlled annealing mechanism to gradually transition from globally optimal patterns to more concentrated solutions, ensuring both efficiency and broad exploration capability.\n\n  The central idea is to transform the optimization into a binary vector selection problem with the constraint that the sum of the elements equals half the length (i.e., sum(h) = n/2). This binary representation captures the essence of the Erd\u0151s problem, where shifts align optimally only with specific binary patterns. By imposing a second constraint (variance control), we bias the distribution to avoid uniformity unless absolutely necessary.\n\n  The annealing process involves three key stages:\n      1. Solve correlated binary systems by balancing sum and energy constraints to capture large-scale patterns.\n      2. Refine solutions with n-point mutations to escape local optima and enhance pattern focus.\n      3. Operate in both binary and low-discrepancy search modes to handle periodicity and inherent symmetries.\n\n  Mathematical specification:\n  We represent the function h as a length-n binary vector (0/1 values) satisfying:\n      E: h \u2208 {0, 1}^n\n      S: \u2211 h[i] = n/2\n      V: (\u2211 h[i]^2) / (n/2) \u2208 [0.5, 2.0]   (controls variance to avoid flat distributions)\n\n  The overlap integral becomes:\n      O = max_{k} [ sum_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[i+k % n]) ]\n\n  Since all computations must be performed modulo the periodic boundary (length n), we ensure all indices are wrapped appropriately.\n\n  Implementation procedure:\n  1. Initialization: Start by interleaving 0/1 patterns across grid points, ensuring minimal local correlation.\n  2. Local cooling: Perform iterative coordinate descent with four annealing rates and period doubling to map solutions across grid resolutions.\n  3. Mutation scheduling: Apply targeted n-point mutations proportional to the objective function's temperature.\n\n  Search choices and useful variations:\n  - Level triggers automatically based on futility (no improvement or goal progression)\n  - Four distinct annealing profiles:\n      1. Global pattern shifting (strong correlation control)\n      2. Local sharpness variation (mutation rate)\n      3. Periodicity enforcement (sample spacing)\n      4. Binary/multi-point switching\n  - Each solution transfers cleanly between grid resolutions using linear interpolation with averaging\n\n  Computebudget and robustness:\n  - The optimization runs serially through levels\n  - Time checkpoints occur at each annealing cycle\n  - On timeout, we return the best complete solution discovered\n\n  Validation and return contract:\n  - All h vectors satisfy h[i] \u2208 {0,1} and sum(h)=n/2\n  - Computed objective matches the reference correlation metric\n  - Function output format: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n  - Includes self-healing capabilities and resolution replication.",
